  (mongodb‑*)                                         became bound.             
                                                                                

Root cause                                                                      

 • Faulty component(s): The application pods (reservation, geo, rate, profile,  
   user, recommendation) repeatedly crashed because they attempted to open a    
   MongoDB connection before the corresponding MongoDB pod was ready.           
 • Why it breaks the application: Each service reads its database URL from the  
   environment (e.g., mongodb-reservation:27017). When the MongoDB pod is not   
   yet running (PVC still provisioning), the driver returns “no reachable       
   servers”, the Go code panics, and the container exits with exitCode = 2.     
   Kubernetes then applies a back‑off restart policy, leading to the observed   
   BackOff events and high restart counts.                                      
 • Evidence:                                                                    
    1 reservation pod JSON shows lastState.terminated.reason: "Error" and       
      exitCode: 2 with logs panic: no reachable servers.                        
    2 Event list contains Back-off restarting failed container for each affected
      pod.                                                                      
    3 PVC provisioning events (ProvisioningSucceeded) occur after the initial   
      start of the dependent pods (≈04:56 UTC), while the MongoDB pods only     
      become ready at the same time.                                            

Remediation suggestions                                                         

 1 Add a readiness probe (or init container) to each service that waits for its 
   MongoDB endpoint to become reachable before starting the main container.     
 2 Ensure PVCs are pre‑provisioned (or use a storage class that provisions      
   instantly) so MongoDB pods start before dependent services.                  
 3 Adjust deployment ordering: set dependsOn via Helm hooks or use postStart    
   scripts to retry DB connections with exponential back‑off instead of         
   panicking.                                                                   

Implementing one or more of these changes will prevent the services from        
crashing on startup and eliminate the BackOff cycles.                           

